NAME      faceFlagsNoCross -- faceT's two faces do not share a body, so F2 and F3
          cannot pass, and its control now reads the same as its assertions.

SEVERITY  The fixture is SILENTLY NON-DISCRIMINATING. That is worse than red.

WHAT      incant/faceT asserts that a field and its "face" share one GroupBody,
          so a flag written through either is visible through the other. Its rows:

              F2  flags FORWARD  write source, read face    want 1   reads 0
              F3  flags REVERSE  write face, read source    want 1   reads 0
              F0  anti-vacuity   never written              control  reads 0

          F2 and F3 have been RED. F0 is their control and it now reads 0 TOO, so
          the control and the assertions are indistinguishable. A fixture whose
          control cannot be told from its subject asserts nothing, whichever way
          the rows go.

⚠ THE CONTROL ONLY BECAME INDISTINGUISHABLE ON 2026-09-08, AND NOT BY BREAKING.
F0 used to read the string `noPrinT` -- an unset flag had no data and returned
its own tag (bear-trap 26). It reads an honest 0 now because opDot gives a
data-less flag read a real count and bareIfTruth makes `if` answer by truthOf.
The row was re-pinned to 0 the same day. So the FIX is what exposed this: the
echo had been hiding the collision between the control and the subject.

WHY THE BODIES DIFFER, and this is the part that makes it someone else's bug.
faceT builds its face in two steps and BOTH COPY:

        fcBag +% fcSrc;              +% attaches a COPY and stamps isAttribute
        fcFace <- fcBag["fcSrc"];    <- MINTS A COPY, it is not an alias

So the face is a copy of a copy. addrOf says so out loud -- FC-1 prints two
different body numbers where faceT's own F1 line says "two fields, ONE body".

⚠ `<-` COPYING IS A RULED DEFECT, NOT THE DESIGN. Tony, 2026-08-23: `<-` is MEANT
to hand back THE NODE, and copy-on-rebind is the defect -- a copy confirms every
read-back of a write that changed nothing. faceT is a downstream casualty of it,
which is why this is filed rather than fixed here.

NEXT: BEST GUESS.

  1. THE LIKELY ANSWER, and it is not this file's to land: faceT cannot work
     while `<-` copies. When copy-on-rebind is fixed, re-run faceT -- F2 and F3
     should go to 1 and F0 must STAY 0. ⚠ If F0 moves to 1 as well, the fix has
     over-shared and the control is telling you so.
     GRADE: strong. The two-copy construction is read directly from the source
     and the differing bodies are printed. What is NOT established is that
     copy-on-rebind is the ONLY reason -- `+%` copies too, so even a fixed `<-`
     leaves one copy between source and face.
  2. ⚠ WHICH MEANS THE CHEAPER TEST COMES FIRST: reach the face WITHOUT `<-`.
     Read it by direct subscript at the point of use rather than rebinding it to
     a local. If the bodies still differ, `+%` is the copy that matters and `<-`
     is exonerated for this fixture.
  3. Failing both, faceT's premise is simply wrong and the fixture wants
     retiring rather than repairing -- roundTripT already pins the same law from
     the other side (ARM B1 copyOf does not cross, ARM B2 addGroup does).

⚠ WHAT MUST NOT HAPPEN: do not re-pin F2 and F3 to 0 to make the fleet green.
That freezes "flags do not cross a face" as the law, which is the opposite of
what the fixture was built to prove, and it would be a target regenerated green.
